Versions
main

Consider this code:
import torch

def inner(i):
    if i == 3:
        torch._dynamo.graph_break()
    return i

@torch.compile(backend="eager")
def fn(x):
    lst = [inner(i) for i in range(5)]
    return x + sum(lst)


inp = torch.randn(3)
fn(inp)

In Python 3.12+, the list comprehension code is inlined into fn, so when we graph break, we end up skipping tracing for all of fn. In previous Python versions, the list comprehension is compiled into its own function object, so we skip tracing only on the list comprehension, but we trace the rest of fn.
The function InstructionTranslatorBase.maybe_has_backedge is responsible for determining when a frame should be skipped due to a graph break in a for loop.
