I am working on integrating a CUDA-like custom backend into PyTorch via the privateuse1 backend extension mechanism. My core design goal for this integration is to achieve full compatibility without making any modifications to PyTorch's source code.
When compiling our custom operator library for this backend with the latest PyTorch versions, a compilation error is thrown.
In file included from /root/tecopytorch210/latest/pytorch/tecopytorch/aten/native/sdaa/cumsum.scpp:29:
In file included from /root/tecopytorch210/latest/pytorch/torch/include/ATen/core/Tensor.h:4:
In file included from /root/tecopytorch210/latest/pytorch/torch/include/ATen/Generator.h:2:
In file included from /root/tecopytorch210/latest/pytorch/torch/include/ATen/core/Generator.h:9:
/root/tecopytorch210/latest/pytorch/torch/include/c10/util/intrusive_ptr.h:191:3: error: static_assert failed due to requirement 'std::atomic<unsigned long>::is_always_lock_free'
  static_assert(std::atomic<uint64_t>::is_always_lock_free);
  ^             ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

My code looks like this:
#include <ATen/Dispatch.h>
#include <ATen/Functions.h>
#include <ATen/core/Tensor.h>
#include <ATen/native/DispatchStub.h>
#include <ATen/native/ReduceOps.h>
#include <aten/dispatch.h>
#include <aten/native/sdaa/ScanKernels.h>
#include <aten/native/sdaa/ScanUtils.h>
// those headers might contains intrusive_ptr.h

#include <cmath>

namespace at::our_device::native {

void launch_cumsum_sdaa_kernel(const TensorBase& result, const TensorBase& self, int64_t dim) {
  AT_DISPATCH_ALL_TYPES_AND2(
      at::ScalarType::Half, at::ScalarType::BFloat16, self.scalar_type(), "cumsum_sdaa", [&]() {
        scalar_t init = 0;
        scan_dim<scalar_t>(self, result, dim, init,
                           [] __device__ (scalar_t a, scalar_t b) -> scalar_t { return a + b; });
      });
}

} 
I guess the failure was caused by compile device code(__device__ marked).After consulting with my colleagues, we have confirmed a hardware limitation specific to our backend: our underlying hardware does not support atomic instructions for 64-bit variables.
Currently, the only workaround to get past this compilation error is to manually comment out a problematic line in PyTorch's intrusive_ptr.h file. But this approach requires modifying PyTorch's native header files, which is what I am trying to avoid.
I'm reaching out to ask:
Are there any valid alternative approaches, compiler flags, or code-level workarounds that would let me resolve this compilation issue and complete the privateuse1 backend integration, without any edits to PyTorch's intrusive_ptr.h or any other PyTorch source/header files?
